{
  "task": "Triage whether the ~33% failure rate on the user's agent's direct calls to the OpenAI API (NOT Bedrock) since this morning is caused by the provider (OpenAI) or by the user's own side (network egress, config, deployment, rate limits, credentials, code). Produce an evidence-based verdict with a confidence level.",
  "context": "The user's agent calls api.openai.com directly for inference. About a third of calls started failing this morning (2026-11-24, UTC; current time ~18:55Z). The user says their own application metrics look normal. They want to know provider vs. us. Only AWS account 012345678901_1 (us-east-1 is the agent space region; check other regions/associated accounts where the workload may run) and its associations are checkable. First discover where the agent runs (Lambda, ECS/EKS, EC2, API Gateway etc.) via topology/log groups/associations. You cannot assume you can reach OpenAI's status page; if you cannot, say so. Use read-only operations only.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 012345678901_1", "agent compute workload (discover it)", "its CloudWatch log groups", "NAT gateways / VPC egress / DNS / security groups / NACLs / proxies on its path", "Secrets Manager/SSM references for the OpenAI API key (metadata only, never read secret values)"],
  "timeframe": "Last ~36 hours up to now (2026-11-24T18:55Z), with baseline = the previous day; pin down the exact onset time of failures.",
  "criteria": "Look for: (1) exact failure onset and shape (steady ~33% vs bursty, by time of day); (2) error breakdown in agent logs: HTTP status codes (429 rate limit / quota, 401/403 auth, 5xx, 408/timeouts), connection resets, TLS errors, DNS failures, and OpenAI error types/messages and request-id headers; (3) whether failures are uniform across instances/AZs/NAT gateways/tasks/models/endpoints or concentrated (concentrated suggests our side, e.g. one NAT/AZ, one pod, one model, one key; uniform with 5xx/overloaded suggests provider); (4) whether 429s are due to our own rate/token limits (increased traffic, retries amplifying, a new prompt size, new model) versus provider-wide; (5) NAT gateway metrics (ErrorPortAllocation, PacketsDropCount, connection counts, bytes), VPC flow log rejects, DNS resolver query failures, ENI/conntrack/port exhaustion; (6) recent changes: deployments, config/env var/secret rotation, SDK/library version bumps, model name changes, IAM/SG/route table/NACL changes (use CloudTrail via the investigating-infrastructure-changes skill), cert/proxy changes; (7) latency/timeout patterns in traces if available; (8) why 'our metrics look normal' may be true (e.g. failures counted as handled/retried, fallback paths, success metrics measured after retry). Report: verdict (provider / us / undetermined) with evidence for and against, onset timestamp, concrete numbers, what could NOT be checked from AWS (e.g. OpenAI-side dashboards, org-level usage limits, OpenAI status page), and 2-3 specific next steps the user can take to confirm (e.g. check OpenAI status page/dashboard rate-limit headers, request ids to cite to OpenAI support)."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Triage OpenAI call failures: check egress, logs, changes in account"}], "created_at": null}

